  <sect2 role="installation">
    <title>Installation of Wine</title>

<!--
    <para>
      First apply an upstream patch so that delay-load import libraries are
      unbroken with <xref linkend="mingw-w64-binutils"/> due to
      Binutils merging .idata sections into read-only .rdata
      sections for PE formatted software upstream:
    </para>

<screen><userinput>patch -Np1 -i ../wine-&wine-version;-avoid_idata_section-1.patch</userinput></screen>
-->

    <para>
      Wine has four build types. Scroll down to the build type you want.
      Visit <xref linkend="winedeps"/> for more information about each
      build type.
    </para>

    <note><para>
      Compiling this package may print out escape sequences due to parallelism.
      Run the <command>reset</command> command if you cannot see what you type
      afterwards or you see any other strange terminal behavior. During
      compilation, it has been observed in some cases that the output font can
      change, whether normal to italic or strikethrough.
    </para></note>

    <sect3>
      <title>WoW64</title>

      <para>
        Install WoW64 Wine by running the following commands:
      </para>

<screen><userinput>mkdir build &amp;&amp;
cd    build &amp;&amp;

../configure --prefix=/usr   \
             --disable-tests \
             --enable-archs=x86_64,i386 &amp;&amp;
make</userinput></screen>

      <para>
        Now, as the &root; user:
      </para>

<screen role="root"><userinput>rm -rf /usr/lib/wine &amp;&amp;
make install</userinput></screen>

    </sect3>

    <sect3>
      <title>Old WoW64</title>

      <para>
        Install the old WoW64 Wine by running the following commands:
      </para>

<screen><userinput>mkdir build-win64 &amp;&amp;
cd    build-win64 &amp;&amp;

../configure --prefix=/usr   \
             --disable-tests \
             --enable-win64 &amp;&amp;

make              &amp;&amp;

cd ..             &amp;&amp;
mkdir build-win32 &amp;&amp;
cd    build-win32 &amp;&amp;

PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/usr/lib32/pkgconfig \
../configure --prefix=/usr           \
             --disable-tests         \
             --with-wine64=../build-win64 &amp;&amp;

make</userinput></screen>

      <para>
        Now, as the &root; user:
      </para>

<screen role="root"><userinput>rm -rf /usr/lib/wine &amp;&amp;
make install         &amp;&amp;
cd ../build-win64    &amp;&amp;
make install</userinput></screen>

    </sect3>

    <sect3>
      <title>64-bit Only</title>

      <para>
        Install 64-bit only Wine by running the following commands:
      </para>

<screen><userinput>mkdir build &amp;&amp;
cd    build &amp;&amp;

../configure --prefix=/usr   \
             --disable-tests \
             --enable-win64 &amp;&amp;

make</userinput></screen>

      <para>
        Now, as the &root; user:
      </para>

<screen role="root"><userinput>rm -rf /usr/lib/wine &amp;&amp;
make install</userinput></screen>

    </sect3>

    <sect3>
      <title>32-bit Only</title>

<!--
      <note><para>
        If you're on 64-bit, invoke
        <userinput>PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/usr/lib32/pkgconfig</userinput> at the
        beginning of the <command>../configure</command> command to build for
        32-bit.
      </para></note>
-->

      <para>
        Install 32-bit only Wine by running the following commands:
      </para>

<screen><userinput>mkdir build &amp;&amp;
cd    build &amp;&amp;

PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/usr/lib32/pkgconfig \
../configure --prefix=/usr --disable-tests &amp;&amp;

make</userinput></screen>

      <para>
        Now, as the &root; user:
      </para>

<screen role="root"><userinput>rm -rf /usr/lib/wine &amp;&amp;
make install</userinput></screen>

    </sect3>

  </sect2>

  <sect2 role="commands">
    <title>Command Explanations</title>

    &options-subconfigure;

    <para>
      <parameter>--disable-tests</parameter>: This parameter disables building
      tests.
    </para>

    <para>
      <parameter>--with-wine64=../build-win64</parameter>: This option
      allows Wine during the compilation stage to use files built in
      the wine64 stage to enable support for both 32-bit and 64-bit.
    </para>

    <para>
      <command>rm -rf /usr/lib/wine</command>: This command removes potentially
      conflicting Wine libraries before installation if Wine has been installed
      previously. Issues normally arise when switching from one build type to
      another. When this is done, Wine will say there's a version mismatch,
      which comes from libraries that shouldn't exist anymore.
    </para>

  </sect2>

  <sect2 role="configuration">
    <title>Configuring Wine</title>

    <para>
      This installation should be good enough for most people but there may be
      a few things that you wish to change or do in relation to this package.
      The <ulink url="https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Wine">Arch Wiki
      page</ulink> for Wine covers most of the things you may wish to do. In
      relation to gaming and Vulkan, you may wish to install
      <xref linkend="dxvk"/> which converts Direct3D 8-11 calls to Vulkan,
      unlike WineD3D which converts Direct3D calls to OpenGL in a poor manner
      and is much slower.
    </para>

    <para>
      As for Direct3D 12, you may also wish to install
      <xref linkend="vkd3d-proton"/> which replaces
      VKD3D provided by Wine. VKD3D is less
      aggressive with optimizations and is geared for general use, whereas
      VKD3D-Proton has aggressive optimizations and
      is geared towards gaming. Despite the name, it can be used with
      Wine and works quite well.
    </para>

    <note><para>
      If you installed the newer WoW64 architecture, you will still need
      32-bit/i686 DLLs for anything extra you wish to install, like DXVK.
    </para></note>

    <para>
      The choice of what to install is up to you.
    </para>

    <para>
      In some cases, some Windows applications will expect files not provided
      in this installation. In such cases, you can install <ulink
      url="&slfs-website;/general/winetricks.html">Winetricks</ulink> from SLFS
      to install those missing files.
    </para>

  </sect2>

